not having a safe and affordable place  to live can play a huge role in the  mistreatment of a child a unlv professor  digs deeper into housing insecurity in a  new study just published she spoke with  victoria saha this morning to talk about  the risks and what would be got what  could be done rather to combat the issue  affordable housing is a need all around  the country but studies show las vegas  is ranked as the most severe when it  comes to the affordable housing shortage  it's really hard to parent appropriately  when you don't have a safe place to  sleep a safe  stimulating environment to raise your  kids catherine marcel a unlv professor  researched the issue of affordable  housing using data from princeton  university which included a 15-year  study which surveyed low-income moms  from 20 different cities really what  we're talking about is a lack of rental  units or homes  that  are going at rates  that low income  to middle income families can  comfortably afford in her new study  called housing insecurity and its risk  to child maltreatment marcel digs into  what the problem looks like well below  the surface living in really overcrowded  housing living in really poor quality  housing you know  rodent infestations holes in the walls  exposed wires  and and just living with really  incredible unaffordability in her study  she found a correlation between housing  insecurity and a child's risk for  physical and psychological abuse they do  not have enough money coming in to keep  up with their basic needs  that means they're only left with bad  options and when you only have bad  options you're going to make a bad  choice she emphasized that this isn't  meant to assume low-income people are  more likely to abuse their children but  how various socioeconomic factors can  contribute to maltreatment of a child  victoria saha 8 news now  now she says the affordable housing  issue can be fixed if we build more  apartments and multi-family homes as  well as put